===Etymology 3===
For {{m|en|testern}}, {{m|en|teston}}, from {{der|en|fr|teston}}, from {{der|en|fro|teste|t=the head, the head of the king being impressed upon the coin}}. See {{m|en|tester|t=a covering}}, and compare {{m|en|testone}}, {{m|en|testoon}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An old [[French#Adjective|French]] [[silver#Adjective|silver]] [[coin#Noun|coin]].
# {{lb|en|Britain|slang|dated}} A [[sixpence]].
#: {{synonyms|en|teston|tizzy}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=S[amuel] R[owlands]|authorlink=Samuel Rowlands|chapter=How a Citizen was Serued by a [[courtesan|Curtizan]]|title=Greenes Ghost Havnting Conie-catchers.{{nb...|Wherein is Set Downe, / the Arte of Humouring. / The Arte of Carrying Stones. / Will. St. Lift. / Ia. Fost. Law. / Ned Bro. Catch. and / Blacke Robins Kindnesse. / With the Conceits of Doctor Pinch-backe a Notable Makeshift. Ten Times More Pleasant than Anything yet Published of This Matter.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed [by [[w:Peter Short (printer)|Peter Short]]?] for R[oger] Iackson, and I. North,{{nb...|and are to be sold in Fleetstreete, a little aboue the Conduit}}|year=1602|oclc=56005075|newversion=republished in|title2=The Complete Works of Samuel Rowlands: 1598–1628: Now First Collected|location2=[Glasgow]|publisher2=Printed [by R. Anderson] for the Hunterian Club|year2=1880|volume2=I|page2=42|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=mbkzAQAAMAAJ&pg=RA5-PA42|oclc2=7106712|passage=When after their beaſtly ſport and pleaſure Mounſieur Libid[inoſo] heat of luſt was ſomewhat aſſwaged, and ready to goe, féeling his pocket for a venereall remuneration [''i.e.'', a copper coin] finds nothing but a '''Teſter''', or at leaſt ſo little, that it was not ſufficient to pleaſe dame Pleaſure for her hire. [...] My Ladie would not beléeue Monſ. Libid. a great while, but ſearched and féeled for more coine, [...]}}

===Etymology 4===
{{inh|en|enm|tester}}; see {{m|en|testiere}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{altform|en|testiere||armor for a horse's head}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2009-01-01|author=Madeleine Pelner Cosman|author2=Linda Gale Jones|title=Handbook to Life in the Medieval World, 3-Volume Set|publisher=Infobase Publishing|isbn=9781438109077|page=275
|text=A horse-head '''tester''' had cutouts for the animal's eyes and ears. A peytral protected the horse's breast, while the crinet, made of overlapping steel plates joined by leather straps or rivets, protected its neck.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Juliet R. V. Barker|title=The Tournament in England, 1100-1400|publisher=Boydell Press|isbn=9780851159423|page=175
|text=The '''tester''' and crupper, together with the piser, which probably protected the chest, formed the basis of most horse armour{{nb...}}}}

